# `operators::expansion::Power::derivative`

[Back to `operators::expansion::Power`](../Power.md)

Differentiates a power series termwise: `c′ₖ = (k + 1) c_{k+1}`.

## Signature

```cpp
operators::Operator operators::expansion::Power::derivative() const;
```

## What It Does

`power().derivative()` acts on a coefficient vector `c = [c₀, c₁, …, c_n]` and returns the coefficients of the derivative series, `[c₁, 2c₂, 3c₃, …, n c_n]`: one entry fewer. The centre does not matter, since the derivative of `Σ cₖ (x − a)ᵏ` has the same form for any `a`. Working on the coefficients avoids building and differentiating the polynomial, and it is exact. The input must be a rank-1 coefficient vector with at least two entries.

## Returns

An `Operator` whose application returns the derivative coefficients, one entry fewer.

## Errors

- `needs a coefficient vector with at least two entries`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The derivative of `1 + 2x + 3x²` is `2 + 6x`: from `[1, 2, 3]` to `[2, 6]`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace expansion = pluss::operators::expansion;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const math::Function c = math::function(pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0, 3.0}));
  out.writeln("derivative coefficients:", operators::expansion::power().derivative()(c)().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2

For `exp`, every coefficient is `1/k!` and the derivative of the series is the series with the last term dropped: the coefficient vector loses one entry and is otherwise unchanged in its leading entries.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace expansion = pluss::operators::expansion;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const math::Function f = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.exp(); });

  const math::Function c = operators::expansion::taylor().coefficients("x", 0.0, 5)(f);
  const math::Function dc = operators::expansion::power().derivative()(c);
  out.writeln("c : ", c().items<double>());
  out.writeln("c': ", dc().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

Consistency with the polynomial: differentiating the coefficients and rebuilding agrees with differentiating the polynomial. Both give `f′` of the order-4 Taylor polynomial of `sin` at `x = 0.7`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace expansion = pluss::operators::expansion;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  const math::Function s = math::function({"x"}, [](math::Function x) { return x.sin(); });
  const math::Function c = operators::expansion::taylor().coefficients("x", 0.0, 4)(s);
  const math::Function by_coefficients = operators::expansion::power("x", 0.0)(operators::expansion::power().derivative()(c));
  const math::Function by_polynomial = operators::differential("x")(operators::expansion::taylor("x", 0.0, 4)(s));
  out.writeln("termwise: ", by_coefficients(0.7).item<double>());
  out.writeln("polynomial:", by_polynomial(0.7).item<double>());
}
```

### Example 4

The input must be a coefficient vector with at least two entries.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

namespace math = pluss::math;
namespace operators = pluss::operators;
using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace expansion = pluss::operators::expansion;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto first_line = [](const pluss::Error &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    return text.substr(0, text.find('\n'));
  };

  const math::Function single = math::function(pluss::make_series({5.0}));
  try { (void)operators::expansion::power().derivative()(single); } catch (const pluss::Error &e) { out.writeln(first_line(e)); }
}
```

